# Copy to .dev.vars before running `npm run preview`.
#
#   cp .dev.vars.example .dev.vars        (bash)
#   Copy-Item .dev.vars.example .dev.vars (PowerShell)
#
# .dev.vars itself is gitignored, because it is where a real secret belongs once this
# project has one. This template is committed so a fresh clone knows the file is expected
# and what goes in it.

# Which of the Next.js env files to load in development. Defaults to "production" when
# unset, which is the wrong default for a local preview.
NEXTJS_ENV=development

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Paid reports (see lib/pricing.ts and app/api/checkout/route.ts)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There is no merchant of record yet: Creem rejected this store, and Anyway / Dodo /
# Polar applications are pending. Until a provider approves the account, the buy button
# falls back to a prefilled order email, which is the behaviour the site had before.
#
# WHEN A PROVIDER APPROVES: set the two checkout URLs below to the product's hosted
# payment link. The checkout route appends `merchant_reference=<order>` to whichever URL is
# set, so the reference in the provider dashboard matches the reference in the order table.
# CHECKOUT_URL_SIGNAL=
# CHECKOUT_URL_PRO=
#
# The webhook route verifies an HMAC-SHA256 signature over "<unix timestamp>.<raw body>"
# sent as `x-payment-signature: t=<ts>,v1=<hex>`. Generate a long random secret and paste
# the same value into the provider's webhook configuration. Without it every webhook is
# rejected with 401, which is the correct failure for an unconfigured deployment.
# PAYMENT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Operator identity (see lib/site.ts and /refund/)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The seller's name and postal address, printed on /pricing/ and /refund/ when set. Empty
# in the repository on purpose: a public repo should not carry a home address, and the
# published page still prints it when the deployment variable exists.
# OPERATOR_IDENTITY=
